\details{
\subsection{Reporter Types}{
\subsection{CucumberReporter}{

The base class for all cucumber reporters. It extends \code{testthat::Reporter}
and adds hooks for step-level and feature-level events:
\itemize{
\item \code{start_feature(feature_name)} - Called before executing a feature
\item \code{end_feature()} - Called after all scenarios in a feature complete
\item \code{start_step(step)} - Called before executing a step
\item \code{end_step(step)} - Called after a step completes
}
}

\subsection{CucumberProgressReporter}{

A concrete implementation that prints step-by-step progress to the console,
similar to testthat's ProgressReporter but with step-level detail:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{Feature: Addition
  ✓ Given I have entered 50 into the calculator
  ✓ And I have entered 70 into the calculator
  ✓ When I press add
  ✓ Then the result should be 120 on the screen
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

}

\subsection{Usage}{
\subsection{With cucumber::test()}{

Pass a reporter instance to \code{cucumber::test()}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# Use cucumber's CucumberProgressReporter for step-level detail
cucumber::test(
  "tests/acceptance",
  reporter = cucumber::CucumberProgressReporter$new()
)

# Use testthat's reporter for scenario-level only (backward compatible)
cucumber::test(
  "tests/acceptance",
  reporter = testthat::ProgressReporter$new()
)

# Silent mode
cucumber::test(
  "tests/acceptance",
  reporter = testthat::SilentReporter$new()
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
